\section*{Introduction}
Federated learning (FL) has emerged as a promising machine learning paradigm that enables collaborative model training while preserving data privacy. Instead of collecting vast amounts of sensitive private data into a single, central location, federated learning brings the training process directly to the data. Despite these unique characteristics, traditional weight-based federated learning can experience performance degradation due to diverse data across multiple clients \cite{zhang2026federated,gao2025fedpc}. Unfortunately, in the healthcare industry, hospitals and other healthcare-related entities follow highly strict data-sharing policies to ensure patient privacy and security in all manners, following GDPR and other frameworks \cite{quazi2024data}, which contradicts any common features or collaboration among different clients. Heterogeneous FL relaxes the conventional FL assumption that all clients share a common model architecture; moreover, it also focuses on the heterogeneity of data distributions, model architectures, network environments, and hardware devices among participating clients \cite{ye2023heterogeneous}. The aggregation problem then shifts from weight-space averaging to how a server can reconcile contributions from, for example, a logistic regression model and a neural network into a coherent global signal. This is precisely where prototype-based FL (PBFL) emerges as a more convenient and secure solution \cite{lee2025heterogeneous, li2023prototype}. In PBFL, clients do not share model weights or raw data but instead share prototypes focusing on class embeddings. However, prototype sharing introduces its own privacy risks, as aggregated prototype vectors can be exploited by an intruder or curious server to infer sensitive characteristics of local datasets \cite{zuo2024privacy}. 
Existing prototype-based heterogeneous FL methods either rely on public datasets for knowledge transfer or expose prototype information to the aggregation server. Consequently, no current approach simultaneously provides arbitrary model heterogeneity (cross-family), proper privacy preservation from the internal entities (server, other clients) and external intruders, secure prototype aggregation, and dropout recovery.\\
In order to mitigate all the existing criticality and security issues, we introduce SAPPHIRE (Secure Aggregation for Privacy-preserving Prototype-based Heterogeneous-fl Integrated with Random-number Embedding), a federated learning protocol that jointly addresses model heterogeneity and cryptographic privacy.

\subsection*{Overview of SAPPHIRE Protocol}
 SAPPHIRE is a privacy-preserving, model-agnostic protocol, where clients may independently select from linear, neural, and tree architectures while ensuring that the aggregation server remains computationally blind to individual client updates. Except for the aggregated global prototype, no individual client's local prototype vector is ever exposed in plaintext to the server or peer clients. It is achieved by employing a unique double-masking strategy using secure multiparty computation (SMPC), combining pairwise Diffie–Hellman masks with individual local masks as part of additive secret sharing and secured by Shamir secret sharing for dropout recovery. The protocol is implemented in Python with authenticated AES-GCM transport over a RabbitMQ message broker and is evaluated on a clinical benchmark dataset, demonstrating a substantially higher balanced accuracy and accurate reconstruction across all tested dropout scenarios.
 
The principal contributions of this work are fourfold.

      \begin{itemize}
          \item SAPPHIRE introduced a prototype aggregation protocol supporting arbitrary heterogeneous models. Clients are fully independent in terms of model architecture from any family: linear, neural, or tree-based architecture, and also in terms of dataset size and feature dimensionality. SAPPHIRE uses a centralized aggregated strategy where nodes share the prototype only with the aggregator node.
          
          \item SAPPHIRE provides privacy preservation, including blind-server guarantees. The only information about any individual client's dataset that the orchestrator can access is the number of classes. All client-specific information is cryptographically secured before it reaches the orchestrator server. On the other hand, it uses a broker-mediated communication architecture, eliminating direct client interaction.
       
          \item SAPPHIRE achieves an high level of security through secure multi-party computation (SMPC) based on a unique double-masking secure aggregation mechanism extending SecAgg to prototype exchange. Each client's prototype update is scrambled by two different masks, both utilizing cryptographically secure pseudorandom numbers (CSPRNG). This secure aggregation protocol is inspired by Flower SecAgg \cite{bonawitz2016practical,li2021secure}, extended by the additional local mask.
       
          \item SAPPHIRE is implemented and evaluated using a real clinical federated learning scenario including an orchestrator, message broker, and multiple clients. The simulation result demonstrates ...
      \end{itemize}
     

