\section*{Related Work}

This section explores some recent research works related to different aspects and variants of federated learning, like heterogeneous FL, prototype-based FL, privacy-preserving and secure data aggregation, etc. We analyze how different prototype-based FL solutions address model heterogeneity and, to what extent, their accuracy rate and fault tolerance. Moreover, we also examine the privacy and security considerations to identify critical security and heterogeneity gaps in existing frameworks that SAPPHIRE is designed to address. 
\subsection*{Heterogenous Federated Learning}
The primary version of federated learning, denoted as \textbf{FedAvg} (Federated Averaging) in \cite{mcmahan2017communication,sun2021decentralized}, was designed to aggregate client model updates (weights) and average them, assuming they share the same architecture. There was no scope for heterogeneity. As a generalization and re-parametrization of FedAvg, \textbf{FedProx} framework introduced heterogeneity in federated networks using a proximal term to limit client drift under non-IID data \cite{li2020federated}. \textbf{SCAFFOLD} uses control variates (variance reduction) to correct the ‘client drift’ and is not affected by data heterogeneity or client sampling. \textbf{FedMD} and \textbf{FedDF} introduced heterogeneous FL via model distillation, using entirely different model architectures and private data \cite{li2019fedmd, lin2020ensemble}. The first one lets clients transfer knowledge via predictions on a shared public dataset rather than weights, and the second one uses unlabeled or synthetic data at the server.

\textbf{PPPML-HMI} is a privacy-preserving personalized FL for heterogeneous healthcare data \cite{zhou2024personalized}. It utilizes PerFedAvg (personalized FedAvg) to allow each healthcare institution to maintain a customized, accurate model tailored to its specific data distributions, and the security is ensured using cyclic secure aggregation combined with homomorphic encryption. Its heterogeneity is at the level of data and device, not model family. \textbf{PHMS-Fed} introduced heterogeneous multi-modal sensor fusion based on federated learning \cite{wang2025privacy}. This framework explicitly handles non-IID data generated across varying, decentralized healthcare institutions. Feature heterogeneity is one of the main contributions of PHMS-Fed, alongside a privacy-preserving feature transformation function, layered with secure parameter aggregation via weighted averaging. \textbf{AAFV} \cite{xu2024privacy} and \textbf{FedSKD} \cite{weng2026fedskd} both provide architecture-agnostic heterogeneity—the former via structure-agnostic federated voting and the later via peer-to-peer knowledge distillation. 

\subsection*{Prototype-based Federated Learning}
\textbf{FedProto} \cite{tan2022fedproto} introduced the foundational framework for prototype-based federated learning for heterogeneous client data, where instead of sharing entire model weights or gradients, clients send abstract, class-level representations called "prototypes" to a central server. Sharing only class prototypes drastically reduces communication overhead and data leakage risks. However, cross-family heterogeneity is not what it demonstrates. \textbf{FedRFQ} is another PBFL method designed to mitigate prototype redundancy and prototype failures across non-IID datasets. It defends against poisoned data, but it does not hide good prototypes from the server, since the server receives raw prototypes \cite{FedRFQ}. \textbf{FedAdapterProto} is a specialized, inherently interpretable PBFL framework designed to analyze retinal fundus images. It addresses statistical data heterogeneity by sending class prototypes and ``lightweight adapters" to the server instead of full weights \cite{ofosu2025prototype}. However, sending adapter weights still requires all clients to share an identical base model architecture (homogeneous models). \textbf{MPCPFL, ProtoMFL, ProMoE-FL} successfully adopt feature heterogeneity by utilizing modality-specific prototypes to bridge the gap between misaligned feature spaces \cite{yu2026mpcpfl, zhang2026protomfl, chhetri2026promoefl}. While effective for structural data variations, they rely entirely on the inherent dimensionality reduction of prototypes for privacy.

\subsection*{Secure Aggregation and Privacy-Preserving FL}
\textbf{SecAgg} is a secure aggregation technique for privacy-preserving FL that allows a central server to compute the sum of individual client updates (e.g., model gradients) without ever seeing the raw, unmasked data of any individual participant using Secure Multi-Party Computation (SMPC) \cite{bonawitz2016practical}. \textbf{LightSecAgg} and \textbf{CESA} are lightweight approaches that significantly reduce the communication overhead needed to recover dropped clients \cite{so2022lightsecagg,nazemi2024boosting}.Protocol like \textbf{FedPropSAG} is a prototype-based aggregation mechanism which is cryptographically secure by combining SecAgg and Differential Privacy to classify MRI brain tumors. Another multi-server protocol, which is also named \textbf{SecAgg} was introduced to explicitly aim for FedAvg-consistent aggregation using additive secret sharing \cite{yang2026secagg}. However, almost all of these highly secure methods force every hospital to use the exact same underlying model backbone to align the features.
