\section*{SAPPHIRE Protocol Architecture}
\subsection*{Priliminaries} 
\begin{itemize}
    
    \item Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange: 
    Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange method is a way of secure communication between two parties to share secret keys without exposing anything to third parties or intruders \cite{rescorla1999diffie}. By using a safe prime and a primitive root, each party generates a public and private key pair using the formula: 
\[(priv\_k \in (0, p),  pub\_k = g^{priv\_k} \bmod p)\]
. This public key is shared between all the entities and used to generate a shared secret for each pair $(i,j) $ as follows:
\[ s_{i,j} = (pub\_k_j)^{priv\_k_i} \bmod p\]. 
By the symmetry of the Diffie-Hellman exchange, both entities compute the same value. This shared secret is then used to encrypt subsequent communication using strong cryptographic techniques.
    \item Cryptographically Secure Pseudorandom Number Generation (CSPRNG): Unlike standard PRNG, CSPRNG is designed to guarantee unpredictability and withstand advanced cryptographic attacks. A deterministic CSPRNG expands a short secret seed into a 
    long output stream that is computationally indistinguishable from true randomness to any party that does not know the seed. Two parties holding the same seed can independently generate the identical stream. SAPPHIRE employs CSPRNG using two mechanisms: HKDF \cite{rfc5869, krawczyk2010hmac}and AES-CTR \cite{kessler2003overview,rfc3686}. 
    
    \item Secure Multi-Party Computation (SMPC): SAPPHIRE utilizes different methods of SMPC:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pairwise additive masking: allows multiple participants to obfuscate their data using a pairwise mask derived from the shared seed, added by one party and subtracted by the other \cite{zhang2025efficient}. Hence, while aggregated, the masks are canceled out automatically, revealing the exact aggregated sum without exposing any individual client's raw data.
        \item Shamir's Secret Sharing (SSS): Shamir's scheme splits a secret into $N$ distinct shares and distributes them among participants. The original key can only be reconstructed if a minimum threshold $t$ of active shares is available. It encodes the secret as the constant term of a degree-$(t-1)$ polynomial and distributes evaluations of that polynomial as shares; reconstruction uses Lagrange interpolation. This threshold structure makes the scheme fault-tolerant by enabling dropout recovery. 
        \item Secure Aggregation (SecAgg): SecAgg is a specialized SMPC protocol designed for federated learning infrastructure that combines both pairwise additive masking (for hiding the individual raw value) and Shamir's Secret Sharing (to recover the masks of dropped clients). It allows the orchestrator server to aggregate model updates in a privacy-preserving manner without revealing any individual data. 
    \end{itemize}

\end{itemize}

\subsection*{System Model} 
The entire SAPPHIRE protocol is based on three major participants:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The clients are the institutional data holders. In the healthcare scenario, the clients can be any hospital, research labs, or any other entity that is in charge of holding the patient record/dataset. Clients are responsible for local model training using their individual dataset for the federated learning purpose. Clients are assumed to be honest participants.
    \item The orchestration server is the central entity responsible for broadcasting the global model update and secure aggregation for federated averaging. It is the sole entity that communicates with all clients in a privacy-preserving manner. The server is assumed honest but curious, and all aggregation and reconstruction operate exclusively on masked values, so the server never handles an individual client's prototype in plaintext.
    \item  The message broker is a stateless relay that acts as a postman, which forwards encrypted messages (like a sealed envelope) between the server and clients; it has no access to the actual contents and takes no part in the overall computation. Every communication in the entire SAPPHIRE protocol is broker-mediated. All communication channels are secured via Transport Layer Security (TLS) to prevent external attacks.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Protocol Phases}
SAPPHIRE operates as a broker-mediated protocol between one orchestrating server and $N$ participating client data holders. All communication is relayed through a RabbitMQ message broker over authenticated AES-GCM channels. To explain the protocol, we are assuming that the clients are different hospitals or healthcare centers containing multiple patients' datasets that have binary diagnoses (malignant and benign) of a disease (e.g., breast cancer). The orchestrator is an entity with large computational resources to coordinate the communication, which has the responsibility to improve diagnosis accuracy but can not access raw patient data due to strict security laws (e.g., research lab or supercomputing center). 

The protocol consists of five sequential phases: the initialization phase is executed only once for the entire session, and after that, four phases run iteratively for $r$ rounds. 

\subsubsection*{Phase 0: Initialization and Registration}
In this initial phase, all the clients get connected to the server, and the whole system gets prepared for the round phases by sharing all the necessary keys and parameters.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Initial parameters: At session startup, the orchestrator server loads a central configuration file defining the expected number of clients ($N$), rounds per session ($R$), expected number of classes (\texttt{num\_classes}), and heterogeneous model groups. It dictates these parameters to the network by broadcasting a \textsc{REGISTER} command.
    \item Client registration: Upon receiving the broadcast, each client validates its local private dataset and replies with a \textsc{register\_reply} including the \texttt{num\_classes}. The server counts the clients (\texttt{num\_clients}) and aborts if $N \neq \texttt{num\_clients}$ or if any \texttt{num\_classes} mismatch is found. The embedding dimension is calculated as:
\[em\_d = \max(8 \times \texttt{num\_classes},\ 16).\]
The factor 8 creates an overcomplete space so heterogeneous models can separate their representations, and the floor of 16 prevents shrinkage for binary datasets. The server then sets the vector size as
\[S = em\_d \times \texttt{num\_classes}.\] 
It also calculates a 2048-bit safe prime $p$, standardized as RFC 3526 2048-bit MODP Group, with g=2, the conventional generator for this group, which generates the large prime-order subgroup \cite{kivinen2003more}.
The orchestrator server broadcasts the tuple $(p,\, g,\, \textit{em\_d},\, S)$ to all clients.
    
    \item Seed generation and sharing: Each client independently generates three secrets: a Diffie-Hellman masking private key $\textit{priv\_k} < p$ for pairwise masks, an independent Diffie-Hellman communication private key $\textit{comm\_priv} < p$ for encrypted Shamir-share channels, and a set of $R$ independent 256-bit local seeds 
$\{{local\_k}_i^{(r)}\}_{r \in [R]}$, one per round.
It then derives the public keys
\[ \textit{pub\_k}   = g^{\textit{priv\_k}}   \bmod p,\]
\[ \textit{comm\_pub} = g^{\textit{comm\_priv}} \bmod p \]
which are broadcast to all participants via the server using RabbitMQ. 
    
    \item {Shamir Secret Sharing:} 
    To ensure fault tolerance, clients split their masking keys and local seeds using Shamir $(t, N)$ secret sharing, with a recovery threshold of $t = \lfloor N/2 \rfloor + 1$. 
  \begin{small}   \[ \{{priv\_share}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{N} = \mathrm{ShamirSplit}_{t,N}\big({priv\_k}_i\big),\]  
  \[ \{{loc\_share}^{(r)}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{N} 
   = \mathrm{ShamirSplit}_{t,N}\big({local\_k}_i^{(r)}\big), 
   \quad \forall r \in [R].\] \end{small}
    These shares are distributed to peers using end-to-end AES-GCM encryption.
   \begin{small} \[share_{i,j} = (comm\_pub_j)^{comm\_priv_i} \bmod p\]
    \[key_{i,j} = \mathrm{HKDF\text{-}SHA256}(share_{i,j})\]
    \[ E_{i,j} = \mathrm{AES\text{-}GCM}_{key_{i,j}}
   \big(priv\_share_{i,j} \,\|\, \{{loc\_share}^{(r)}_{i,j}\}_{r \in [R]} \big)\] \end{small}
    The server maintains a \emph{share escrow}, a routing table indexed by destination client, into which it files every incoming ciphertext $E_{i,j}$ under the identifier of its intended recipient $j$. 
    
   \item {Pairwise and local seed derivation:}
Finally, clients derive pairwise masking seeds using Diffie-Hellman key exchange and self-masking seeds via independent per-round sampling.
\[(pub\_k)_{i,j} = \textit{pub\_k}_j ^ {\,\textit{priv\_k}_i} \bmod p. \] 
The pairwise masking seed is then obtained as:
\[ seed_{i,j} = \mathrm{HKDF\text{-}SHA256}((pub\_k)_{i,j}).\]
Additionally, each client $i$ samples $R$ independent 256-bit seeds $\{\textit{local\_k}_i^{(r)}\}, {r \in [R]} $ at initialization, where $R$ bounds the number of rounds in a session. Round $r$ employs $\textit{local\_k}_i^{(r)}$ as its self-mask seed. All $R$ seeds are Shamir-split during Phase 0 and transmitted in a single encrypted message per peer.
    
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Phase 1: Local Fedproto Training}
At the start of each round $r$, the server broadcasts the current global prototype vector $G_r$ to all clients. If no training model is specified in the configuration, a client selects its own model and trains it on its private dataset. This phase is fully local, and no model updates leave the client.
\begin{itemize}
    \item {Model-Specific Forward Operations:} Clients train their chosen model (linear, neural, or tree) on their private dataset using minibatches. The forward pass is model-specific, using each batch, $X_b$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{Linear Models:} For \texttt{logistic regression} variants, $X_{b}$ is projected directly into the $em\_d$ space. The \texttt{logistic\_bn} variant applies a layer normalization step immediately after projection to stabilize the gradient flow across the batch.
        \item \textit{Neural Models:} For the Deep \texttt{MLP}, the batch passes through two hidden layers with a 20\% neuronal dropout applied during training to prevent local overfitting. For the \\texttt{Transformer Lite}, the numerical features of $X_{b}$ are first expanded into a sequence of individual tokens (\texttt{unsqueeze(-1)}). These tokens are processed by a multi-head self-attention mechanism to capture feature interdependencies before being flattened into the final vector $v$.
        \item \textit{Tree Models:} For the \texttt{Soft Decision Tre}, $X_{b}$ is routed through the tree nodes using temperature-scaled sigmoid functions, outputting a probability distribution over the leaf nodes to compute $v$. For the \texttt{Random Forest} ensemble, $X_{b}$ first undergoes a 30\% feature-level dropout (\texttt{Dropout(p=0.3)}) before being passed to five independent soft trees. This feature bagging ensures the trees learn diverse representations, which are then averaged to produce the final batch embeddings.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Local FedProto Loop: 
      Each client optimizes a composite objective composed of two different loss terms: (1) classification loss and (2) prototype alignment loss.
    The first one is a standard cross-entropy classification loss ($\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}$), that pushes the model to fit the client's own data well. The second is a prototype alignment loss $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}$ that pulls each sample's normalized embedding toward the global prototype for its class. The total loss is computed by simply adding the two kinds of loss together: 
\[ \mathcal{L}_{total} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}} + \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
    = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}} + \frac{1}{b} \sum_{k=1}^{b} \lambda_{y_k} \left\| v_k - g_{y_k} \right\|^2_2, \]
  where,      
\[\lambda_c = \mathrm{clip}\!\left( \frac{\kappa}{\sqrt{n_c}},\; 
        \lambda_{\min},\, \lambda_{\max} \right).\]  
        This alignment loss ($\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}$) is the critical mechanism that enables true cross-family model heterogeneity. The global prototypes ($g_{y_k}$) act as universal geometric anchors. By continuously penalizing the $L_2$ distance between local embeddings and these global anchors, the composite loss forces structurally disparate models to independently learn mappings into the same shared coordinate space ($em\_d$) without requiring homogeneous feature extractors.

   \item Prototype Extraction \& Quantization:
    After training, the client computes the $L_2$-normalized mean embedding per class:
    \[ l_{r_i} = \mathrm{norm}\left( \frac{1}{n_c} \sum_{y_k=c} \mathrm{norm}(v_k) \right) \]
    These extracted prototypes are flattened into a one-dimensional array and multiplied by a fixed quantization scale ($Q = 100,000$). To prevent missing-class dilution during aggregation, the client appends a binary Class Presence Vector ($c_i$) indicating which classes exist in its local dataset, forming the final payload array:
    \[  l'_{r_i} = \text{round}(l_{r_i} \times Q) \,\|\, c_i \] 
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Phase 2: Secure Double Masking} 

For every round $r$, client $i$ generates two masks: one pairwise mask and one private mask using a secure multi-party computation (SMPC) to its quantized array $l'_r$ using a cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator (CSPRNG) to expand the seeds into dense noise vectors:
\begin{small}
\[\text{mask}_{i,j} = \text{AES-CTR}_{seed_{i,j}}(r, \, length=S) \pmod M \]
\[\text{mask}_i = \text{AES-CTR}_{{local\_k}_i^{(r)}}(r, \, length=S) \pmod M\]
\end{small}
Here, $seed_{i,j} = \mathrm{HKDF\text{-}SHA256}(pub\_k_i^{priv\_k_j} \bmod p)$ and $seed_i = local\_k_i^{(r)}.$ Both masks are freshly generated in every round: the pairwise masks vary with the round index, while the self-mask additionally uses a different seed ${local\_k}_i^{(r)}$ each round. Modulo $M$ is defined to be a finite field size  large enough to accommodate the maximum possible quantized sum without overflow.

The final securely masked array for client $i$, denoted as $L_{r_i}$, is computed as: 
\begin{scriptsize}
    \[ L_{r} = \left( l'_{r_i} + \sum_{j \in \mathcal{U},\, j > i} mask_{i,j} 
- \sum_{j \in \mathcal{U},\, j < i} mask_{i,j} + mask_i \right) \bmod M \]
\end{scriptsize}


Once calculated, client $i$ transmits this highly obfuscated array $L_{r}$ to the orchestrator server.

\subsubsection*{Phase 3: Secure Aggregation \& Dropout Recovery}
 Upon receiving masked updates, the server starts client counting for a prespecified time period. If thr total number of clients that have arrived, $\texttt{num\_survivor} < t$, the round is aborted, since secure reconstruction would no longer be possible. Otherwise, the server sums all received masked arrays:
       \begin{small}  
        \[ \sum sum_{init}  = \Big( \sum_{i \in \mathcal{U}} L_{r} \Big) \bmod M.\]
        \end{small}
Every pairwise mask shared between two surviving clients appears once with each sign and cancels itself out. To clear these remaining masks, the server broadcasts an \textsc{unmask} request naming the survivor set $\mathcal{S}$ and the dropped set $\mathcal{D}$. Each surviving client replies with (1) the shares of every \emph{survivor's} local seed, and (2) the shares of every \emph{dropped} client's masking private key. It reconstructs the DH private key, $priv\_k_d$ and recomputes the pairwise seed and expands it in the exact same pairwise mask, $mask_{s,d}$.
   
        \[ \sum mask_{s,d} = \sum_{d \in \mathcal{D}} \left( 
   \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S},\, s > d} mask_{s,d} 
 - \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S},\, s < d} mask_{s,d} \right) \bmod M \]

The server also reconstructs each survivor's local seed for the current round, 
${local\_k}_s^{(r)}$, by Lagrange interpolation:
        
        \[ \sum mask_s = \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \mathrm{PRNG}\big({local\_k}_s^{(r)}\big) 
        \bmod M.\]
 Finally, the server mathematically strips these masks from the initial blind summation.
    \[ sum_{final} = \Big( sum_{init} - \sum mask_{s,d} - \sum mask_s \Big) \bmod M. \]
    This final operation reveals the true, unmasked sum of the quantized local prototypes for the active network.

\subsubsection*{Phase 4: Federated Averaging \& Global Model Update}

Once the secure aggregation has successfully recovered the raw summed array, the server maps $sum_{final}$ from the positive finite field back to signed integers using a centered modulo operation to handle negatives:
    \[ sum_{signed} = \Big((sum_{final} + \lfloor M/2 \rfloor) \bmod M \Big) - \lfloor M/2 \rfloor \]
To update the global prototypes, the server divides the summed prototype coordinates by the number of clients that contributed to each class, as indicated by the secure Class Presence Vector ($C_c$), rather than by the total number of surviving clients. This prevents rare classes from being artificially diluted toward zero by clients missing those specific features:
   $$G_{r+1} = \frac{sum_{signed\_protos}}{\max(C_c, 1) \times Q}$$
This matrix is $L_2$ normalized to become the newly updated global knowledge prototype. Round $r$ concludes with the orchestrator server broadcasting $G_{r+1}$ back to the client network.

