\section*{Security and Privacy Analysis}
To prove the effectivness of the SAPPHIRE protocol, we analyze its defenses against common security threats in federated networks. The protocol is carefully designed to ensure data confidentiality, system availability, and fault tolerance.

\subsubsection*{Privacy from orchestrator} The orchestrator server never receives local prototypes in plaintext because of the strict double masking method where the plain prototype update is obfuscated twice using cryptographic noise ($mask_{i,j}$ and $mask_i$). The server only ever sees the final aggregated result $sum_{final}$. Finally, because each round uses an independently sampled local seed $\{{local\_k}_i^{(r)}\}$, the system achieves perfect forward secrecy, ensuring past and future updates remain cryptographically sealed.

\subsubsection*{Protection from network interception}
To prevent the broker or external attackers from accessing the confidential medical data, all peer-to-peer secrets (such as the Shamir shares) are encrypted end-to-end using authenticated AES-GCM. Furthermore, Transport Layer Security (TLS) protects the communication tunnels from eavesdropping. The broker functions purely as a postman, seeing only encrypted envelopes.

\subsubsection*{Resilience to Client Dropouts}
In case of unexpected client dropout, SAPPHIRE guarantees system liveness through Shamir's Secret Sharing.  As long as the number of surviving clients meets the minimum threshold $t = \lfloor N/2 \rfloor + 1$, the server can accurately reconstruct the missing cryptographic masks of the dropped clients. It eliminates their noise from the total sum without ever exposing their actual raw data, ensuring the global model continues to train uninterruptedly.


