\section*{Results \& Discussion}

To evaluate the practical viability, cryptographic overhead, and fault tolerance of the SAPPHIRE protocol, we executed federated simulations with one orchestrator server and four simulated healthcare clients communicating via a RabbitMQ message broker.
%==============================
\subsection*{Experimental Setup}
To simulate the SAPPHIRE framework, we implemented the protocol using Python, utilizing PyTorch for the local machine learning models and the \texttt{cryptography} library for secure multi-party computation operations. Network communication between the orchestrator server and the clients was coordinated through a RabbitMQ message broker. The protocol was tested on a binary classification task (identifying benign versus malignant diagnoses). The federated network was configured with one central orchestrator and four independent client nodes ($N=4$). The training session was set to run for 10 global communication rounds ($R=10$). 
To simulate a realistic multi-center healthcare environment, the four independent client nodes ($N=4$) were each assigned a distinct clinical breast cancer dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset that contains features computed from digitized images of fine needle aspirates (FNA) of breast masses \cite{breast_cancer_wisconsin},
    \item  Mammographic Mass dataset consisting BI-RADS attributes and patient age extracted from mammography screening exams, providing a heterogeneous mix of ordinal and integer features \cite{mammographic_mass_161},
    \item  Breast Cancer Coimbra dataset comprising clinical features derived from routine blood analysis (such as glucose, insulin, and resistin levels) \cite{breast_cancer_coimbra_451} and
    \item  Ljubljana Breast Cancer dataset with categorical clinical instances (such as tumor size, node status, and age groups) \cite{breast_cancer_14}.
\end{enumerate}

